        "Why this matters. Writers choose words on purpose. A noun tells the reader who or what is in the story. A verb makes the action move. An adjective helps the reader picture it. When you can notice these jobs, you can make your own sentences clearer, stronger and more interesting.\n\n"
        "The three word jobs. A noun is a naming word: a person, place, animal, object or idea. A verb is a doing or being word: it tells what happens or what something is. An adjective is a describing word: it tells more about a noun. In the sentence 'The tiny bird flew', bird is the noun, flew is the verb and tiny is the adjective.\n\n"
